<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 3 Practice</title>
<p>
    <em>Given a statement to be proven using a direct proof, identify the assumptions to be made and the statements to be proven.</em>
  </p>

  <p>
    See the box on page 52 of your textbook for the outline of a direct proof.
    If we are trying to prove <m>P\implies Q</m>, i.e., a statement of the form
    <q>if <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m> </q>, we assume <m>P</m> and show <m>Q</m>.
  </p>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Practice</title>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Examples with solutions (at bottom of page). For each, state what
            you would assume in a direct proof and state what you would prove in
            a direct proof.
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  For all integers <m>n</m>, if <m>n</m> is an odd integer, then
                  <m>n^{2}+1</m> is even.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  For all integers <m>x</m>, if <m>x</m> is divisible by
                  <m>6</m>, then <m>x</m> is divisible by <m>3</m>.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  For all natural numbers <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>, if <m>p</m> and
                  <m>q</m> are twin primes other than <m>3</m> and <m>5</m>,
                  then <m>pq+1</m> is a perfect square and <m>36</m> divides
                  <m>pq+1</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            More practice:
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  For all real numbers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, if <m>x&gt;y</m>
                  then <m>x-7&gt;y-7</m>.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  For all integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, if <m>a</m> and
                  <m>b</m> are both even, then <m>4</m> divides <m>ab</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

</paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Solutions</title>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Since the statement starts ``for all'', we should start with an
            arbitrary one. So, assume <m>n</m> is an integer. We also assume the
            hypothesis in a direct proof, so assume <m>n</m> is an odd integer.
            Prove that <m>n^{2}+1</m> is even.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Again since the statement starts ``for all'', we should start with
            an arbitrary one. So assume <m>x</m> is an integer. Also assume the
            hypothesis, so assume <m>x</m> is divisible by <m>6</m>. Then prove
            that <m>x</m> is divisible by <m>3</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Assume <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> are natural numbers, and that <m>p</m>
            and <m>q</m> are twin primes other than <m>3</m> and <m>5</m>. Prove
            that <m>36</m> divides <m>pq+1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
</paragraphs>
</handout>